// the levels of learnj built into the program (generated from levels.txt and levels_en.txt by gen_levels.sh)
String embedded_levels(bool english) {
    String r = "";
    if english {
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tWhat is a computer\tA computer is a machine that takes in data, works on it by following instructions, and gives out a result.~It does simple things, such as adding, comparing and moving data, very fast.~A phone, a laptop, a game console and a cash machine are all computers.\tWha";
    r = r + "t does a computer mainly do?\ttakes data in, processes it by instructions, gives a result\tonly draws pictures\tstores electricity\t0\tInput, processing, output.\tWhich of these is a computer?\ta mobile phone\ta glass of water\ta ruler\t0\tA phone has a CPU and programs.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tHardware and software\tHardware is the parts you can touch: the CPU, the memory, the screen, the battery.~Software is the programs and data that tell the hardware what to do.~The same machine does new work when you change the software.\tWhat is a CPU?\thardware\tsoftware\tdata\t0\tThe";
    r = r + " CPU is a real part.\tWhat is a program?\tsoftware\thardware\ta cable\t0\tA program is software.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tThe CPU: the part that follows instructions\tThe CPU follows instructions one by one: it fetches one, works out what it means, does it, and goes to the next.~Its speed is given in gigahertz: 3 GHz is about 3 billion steps a second.~Several cores can do several jobs at the same t";
    r = r + "ime.\tHow does a CPU follow instructions?\tone after the other\tall at once\tat random\t0\tFetch, decode, do, next.\tWhat do several cores give you?\tseveral jobs at the same time\ta bigger screen\tfile storage\t0\tEach core runs its own job.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tMemory (RAM)\tRAM holds the data and programs that are in use. It is fast, but it is emptied when the power goes off.~Every cell of RAM has an address; the CPU reads and writes by address.~When RAM is full the machine has to throw out or move what is in use.\tWhat happens to the ";
    r = r + "data in RAM when the power goes off?\tit is lost\tit stays for ever\tit goes to the internet\t0\tRAM is temporary memory.\tHow does the CPU find data in RAM?\tby address\tby file name\tby colour\t0\tEvery cell has an address.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tPermanent storage\tPermanent storage (the internal memory, an SD card) keeps files when the power is off, but it is much slower than RAM.~A program must be read from storage into RAM before it can run.~Files are kept by name in folders.\tHow is storage different from RAM?\tit keep";
    r = r + "s data when off, but it is slower\tit is faster and empties when off\tno difference\t0\tPermanent but slow.\tWhen can a program run?\tafter it is read into RAM\tonly while it is in storage\tnever\t0\tIt has to be in RAM first.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tInput and output\tInput devices: the keyboard, the touch screen, the microphone, the camera.~Output devices: the screen, the speaker, the vibration motor, the network.~A program talks to the devices through the operating system.\tWhat kind of device is a microphone?\tinput\toutput\t";
    r = r + "storage\t0\tIt brings sound into the machine.\tWhat kind of device is a speaker?\toutput\tinput\ta CPU\t0\tIt sends sound out.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tData and programs\tData is what a program works on: numbers, text, pictures, sound.~A program is the list of instructions that says what to do with the data.~Inside the machine both are binary numbers; they differ in how they are used.\tWhich of these is data?\ta photo\tan if state";
    r = r + "ment\ta CPU\t0\tA photo is data.\tInside the machine, how do programs and data differ?\tin how they are used\tthey are not numbers\tthe kind of electricity\t0\tBoth are binary numbers.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tThe operating system\tThe operating system (Android, Linux, Windows) manages the machine for all the programs.~It shares the memory, schedules the CPU, looks after the files and controls the devices.~A program asks it for a service with a system call.\tWhat does the operating sys";
    r = r + "tem do?\tmanages memory, CPU, files and devices for the programs\tdraws pictures\tsells things\t0\tIt is the manager of the machine.\tHow does a program ask to read a file?\twith a system call\tby guessing\tby switching off\t0\tIt asks the operating system.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tFiles and folders\tA file is named data. A folder holds files and other folders.~A path gives the place of a file, such as home/notes/a.txt.~The extension (.jk .txt .png) tells what kind of file it is.\tWhat is a path?\tthe place of a file in the folders\ta kind of CPU\ta cable\t0\tIt";
    r = r + " says where the file is.\tWhat does .jk tell you?\ta J2K source file\ta picture\ta sound\t0\tThe extension tells the kind of file.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tThe internet and networks\tA network is several computers that send messages to each other.~Every machine has an address (IP); data is sent in small pieces called packets.~The web uses HTTP: a client asks, a server answers.\tWhat does a server do on the web?\tanswers the requests ";
    r = r + "of clients\tonly keeps pictures\tasks everybody for data\t0\tThe client asks, the server answers.\tWhat is a packet?\ta small piece of data sent over a network\ta cable\ta program\t0\tData is cut into pieces.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Computers: the basics\tHow a program runs\tYou write the program as text (the source code); the compiler turns it into instructions of the CPU.~The operating system reads the program file into RAM and lets the CPU start at main.~When it ends, the operating system takes back the resources.\tWhat does a ";
    r = r + "compiler do?\tturns source code into instructions of the CPU\tdraws the screen\tsends email\t0\tIt translates for the machine.\tWho starts the program at main?\tthe operating system lets the CPU start\tthe user, one instruction at a time\tthe screen\t0\tThe OS loads it and starts at main.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tInstall and run the first file\tThe compiler jcmp turns a .jk file into a program that runs.~Write hello.jk, build it with  jcmp hello.jk -o hello  and run  ./hello .~If you mistype, the compiler tells you the file, the line and the column of the mistake.\tWhich command builds hello.jk ";
    r = r + "into a program?\tjcmp hello.jk -o hello\trun hello.jk\t./hello.jk\t0\tjcmp FILE -o NAME builds it, then you run ./NAME.\tThe compiler reports an error. What do you do first?\tChange the phone\tRead the file, line and column it gives\tDelete the file\t1\tThe message tells you exactly where the mistake is.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tmain and jout\tEvery program starts at the function main.~jout << text prints on the screen, and \\n starts a new line.~You can print several things in a row with several <<.\tWhere does a program start?\tIn the function main\tOn the last line\tIn a function called start\t0\tmain is the start";
    r = r + "ing point.\tWhat does \\n mean inside a text?\tEnd the program\tStart a new line\tDelete the text\t1\t\\n is a new line.\tWrite a program that prints Hello J2K and a new line#Hello J2K~";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tVariables\tA variable is a name for a place that holds a value. It has a type: int whole number, double decimal number, bool true or false, String text.~Make one with  int n = 5;  and change it with  n = 6; .~The type tells what it can hold and how much room it takes.\tWhich type holds ";
    r = r + "a decimal number?\tint\tdouble\tbool\t1\tdouble holds decimal numbers.\tAfter  int n = 5;  and  n = n + 1;  what is n?\t5\t6\t51\t1\tAdding one gives 6.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tCalculate and compare\tThe signs + - * / % calculate. For int, / drops the fraction and % is the remainder.~Compare with == != < > <= >=; the result is a bool.~&& means and, two bars mean or, ! means not.\tWhat is 7 / 2 for int?\t3\t3.5\t4\t0\tInteger division drops the fraction.\tWhat is 7 %";
    r = r + " 2?\t1\t3\t0\t0\t% is the remainder: 7 = 3 x 2 + 1.\tPrint the result of (3 + 4) * 5#35~";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tif\tif condition does one thing, else does another.~else if adds more conditions.~A condition is a bool value, for example from a comparison.\tIf 5 > 3 is true, which block runs?\tthe if block\tthe else block\tnone\t0\t5 > 3 is true, so the if block runs.\tWhen does else run?\talways\twhen the ";
    r = r + "condition of the if is false\tnever\t1\telse is the case where the if is not true.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tLoops\tfor i in 0..n repeats n times (i goes from 0 to n-1).~while condition repeats as long as the condition is true.~Loops are where a program spends almost all its time.\tHow many times does  for i in 0..3  run?\t3\t4\t2\t0\ti = 0, 1, 2.\tA while loop stops when...\tthe condition becomes fa";
    r = r + "lse\tafter 10 turns\tnever\t0\tIt stops when the condition is false.\tPrint the sum of 1 to 10#55~";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tInput with jin\tjin >> variable reads from the keyboard; the type of the variable decides what is read.~Test with a pipe:  echo 5 | ./prog .~When the input is finished, nothing new is read.\tHow can you test a program that reads input without typing?\techo 5 | ./prog\t./prog 5 5\tyou canno";
    r = r + "t\t0\tSend the input with a pipe.\tjin >> n; with n an int reads...\ta whole number\ttext\ta picture\t0\tThe type of the variable decides.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tFunctions\tA function is a named group of statements. It takes arguments and gives back a value:  int square(int x) { return x * x; } .~You call it with square(7).~Small functions make code easy to read and to test.\tWhat does return do?\tgives back a value and ends the function\tprints t";
    r = r + "he value\tdeletes a variable\t0\treturn gives the value to the caller.\tWhat does void mean?\tno value is returned\ta decimal is returned\tfailure\t0\tvoid means it returns nothing.\tWrite a function square(int x) and print square(7)#49~";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tArrays\tAn array keeps many values of one type:  int[] a = arr(4); a.push(10);  a[0] is the first, a.len is the count.~The index starts at 0. An index that is too big is a mistake.~Combine arrays with loops to work on every item.\tWhich item is a[0]?\tthe first\tthe last\tnone\t0\tIndexes st";
    r = r + "art at 0.\tAn array has 3 items. What is the last index?\t3\t2\t1\t1\t0, 1, 2.\tMake an array 1 2 3 4 5 and print the sum#15~";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Write programs\tStrings\tA String is text. Join with +, get the length with .len, compare with ==.~Turn it into a number with .to_int().~\"42\".to_int() gives 42.\tWhat does \"ab\" + \"cd\" give?\tabcd\tab cd\t6\t0\t+ joins texts.\tWhat is \"hello\".len?\t5\t4\t6\t0\tIt has 5 letters.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Data structures\tCounting steps: Big-O\tWe count steps, we do not time the program.~Looking at every item takes n steps (O(n)); halving the range takes log n steps.~The steps grow in the shape of this rule when the data grows.\t1,048,576 items searched by halving take about how many steps?\t20\t1,000,000";
    r = r + "\t100\t0\tlog2 of 1,048,576 is 20.\tO(n) means...\tthe steps grow with the size of the data\talways the same steps\tthe steps grow with the square\t0\tn items, n steps.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Data structures\tStack\tA stack: last in, first out (LIFO).~push puts an item on, pop takes the top one off.~Used to check brackets, for undo, and for function calls.\tLIFO means...\tlast in, first out\tfirst in, first out\trandom\t0\tLast In First Out.\tWhat is a stack good for?\tchecking that brackets are b";
    r = r + "alanced\tsorting\tsending files\t0\tThe last opened bracket must be closed first.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Data structures\tQueue\tA queue: first in, first out (FIFO).~A ring buffer keeps it in a fixed array and wraps the position with %.~Keyboards and network cards use ring buffers.\tFIFO means...\tfirst in, first out\tlast in, first out\tno order\t0\tFirst In First Out.\tWhat does % do in a ring buffer?\twraps t";
    r = r + "he position around\tmultiplies\tsubtracts\t0\t(head + count) % cap wraps around.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Data structures\tSearch tree\tA search tree keeps smaller keys on the left and bigger ones on the right.~Reading left, node, right gives the keys in order.~Search and insert take about log n steps when the tree is balanced.\tReading left, node, right gives...\tthe keys in order\tthe keys mixed\tnothing\t0\t";
    r = r + "That is a sort.\tKeys 10, 20, 30, 40 inserted in this order give...\ta line of height 4\ta balanced tree of height 2\tan empty tree\t0\tSorted input makes a line.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Data structures\tGraph and BFS\tA graph is nodes joined by edges.~Breadth-first search visits nodes by distance from the start, using a queue; the first time it reaches a node is a shortest path.~A node that cannot be reached has distance -1.\tBFS uses which structure?\ta queue\ta stack\ta tree\t0\tThe queu";
    r = r + "e gives the order of distance.\tThe first time BFS reaches a node, the path is...\tthe shortest\tthe longest\trandom\t0\tNodes are visited by distance.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Data structures\tDynamic programming\tDP solves each small case once, writes the answer in a table and builds the big answer from the table.~Coins 1, 5, 12 for 15: greedy needs 4 coins, the table finds 3.~The table turns exponential work into polynomial work.\tThe fewest coins of 1, 5, 12 for 15?\t3\t4\t2";
    r = r + "\t0\t5 + 5 + 5.\tWhat does DP do to be faster?\tremembers the answers of small cases\tguesses\trepeats work\t0\tIt never computes the same case twice.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Data structures\tHash table\tA hash table finds a value from its key in about one step: a function turns the key into a place.~Two keys can want the same place (a collision); open addressing tries the next place.~A nearly full table is slow, so it grows at about 70 % full.\tWhat is a collision?\ttwo key";
    r = r + "s want the same place\tthe program hangs\tmemory is full\t0\tThe hash function gave the same place.\tWhy does a hash table grow?\ta nearly full table has many collisions\tto look nicer\tit does not\t0\tMore collisions means slower.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Data structures\tSorting\tInsertion sort takes about n*n/4 comparisons; merge sort takes about n*log2(n).~For 1000 items: 242,690 against 8,670 comparisons.~For a few items insertion sort is faster; real libraries use both.\tFor many items merge sort is faster because it takes...\tn log n steps\tn x n st";
    r = r + "eps\trandom steps\t0\tlog n grows slower than n.\tDo the two sorts give the same result?\tyes\tno\tit depends\t0\tOnly the number of steps differs.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "The machine and speed\tWhat a computer can do\tA computer does simple things again and again, very fast: add, subtract, compare, move data.~A program is a list of small instructions in order.~Its strength is speed, not cleverness.\tWhere does the speed of a computer come from?\tcleverness\tsimple instruc";
    r = r + "tions done very fast again and again\tguessing\t1\tThe strength is the speed of simple instructions.\tWhat is a program?\ta list of small instructions in order\ta picture file\tthe name of the CPU\t0\tA program is a list of instructions.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "The machine and speed\tBits and bytes\tAll data is 0 and 1, called bits. Eight bits are one byte (0 to 255).~The number 5 in base two is 101; the letter A is stored as 65.~The same bits can mean different things, depending on how the program reads them.\tHow many bits in a byte?\t4\t16\t8\t2\tOne byte is 8 ";
    r = r + "bits.\tWhat is 5 in base two?\t11\t101\t555\t1\t5 = 4 + 1.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "The machine and speed\tVariables, arrays and addresses\tA variable is a name for a place in memory; every place has an address.~An array is many values next to each other: address of item i = start + i x size.~A float takes 4 bytes, a double 8.\tHow many bytes does an array of 1000 floats take?\t1000\t80";
    r = r + "00\t4000\t2\t1000 x 4.\tWhy can item i be reached in one step?\tits address is computed with a multiply and an add\tby counting one by one\tby guessing\t0\tBecause the items are next to each other.\tMake an array 1 2 3 4 5 and print the sum#15~";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "The machine and speed\tSlow RAM and the cache\tThe CPU is much faster than RAM; waiting for RAM costs a hundred additions.~The cache is a small fast memory next to the CPU that keeps recent data.~RAM sends data in blocks of 64 bytes (cache lines).\tWhat is the cache for?\tto keep files for ever\tto reduc";
    r = r + "e the waiting for RAM\tto make the screen brighter\t1\tIt keeps recent data close to the CPU.\tIn what unit does RAM send data?\tone bit\ta block of 64 bytes\tone file\t1\tA cache line.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "The machine and speed\tWalking in order or jumping\tA loop that walks an array in order uses a whole block: fast.~A loop that jumps gets one number per block and throws the block away.~A table is kept in rows: along a row is fast, down a column is slow, same result.\tSumming a table by rows or by colum";
    r = r + "ns: is the sum different?\tvery different\tthe same, but the time can differ\trows are wrong\t1\tSame result, different time.\tWhy is walking down a column slow?\tone number per block, then the block is thrown away\tadding is harder\tRAM is full\t0\tMost of every block is wasted.\tSum a 4 x 4 table (values 1 to";
    r = r + " 16) kept in one array, walking along the rows, and print the sum#136~";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "The machine and speed\tijk and the matrix product\tC = A x B: c[i][j] is the sum of a[i][k] x b[k][j].~The loops i j k can be swapped in 6 ways: same result, different time.~The orders whose inner loop walks along a row (such as ikj) are fast and can use vectors.\tIn how many ways can the loops i j k b";
    r = r + "e ordered?\t3\t6\t9\t1\t3 x 2 x 1.\tWhich order is often faster?\tthe one whose inner loop walks along a row, such as ikj\talways ijk\tno difference\t0\tIt uses the cache and vectors.\tMultiply the 2 x 2 matrices A = 1 2 3 4 and B = 5 6 7 8 and print c00 c01 c10 c11 with spaces#19 22 43 50~";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "The machine and speed\tReview of the week\tReview: the machine does simple things again and again / data is 0 and 1 / the CPU is fast, RAM is slow.~Data in good order is faster / GEMM is the matrix product.~Speed = less waiting for data + calculating many values at once.\tWhat is the heart of making a ";
    r = r + "program fast?\tbuying a more expensive phone\tless waiting for data and many values at once\twriting longer code\t1\tThese two things.\tWhat is GEMM?\ta game\tthe general matrix product\ta cache name\t1\tGeneral matrix multiply.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Language: English\tGreetings and introductions\tGreet: Hello, Good morning, Good evening.~Introduce yourself: My name is Anna. I am from Thailand.~Ask a name: What is your name?\tWhen do you say Good morning?\tin the morning\tin the evening\tat night\t0\tmorning is the first part of the day.\tWhich sentence ";
    r = r + "introduces you?\tMy name is Anna.\tWhere is the bus?\tIt is raining.\t0\tMy name is ... gives your name.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Language: English\tThe present simple\tThe present simple tells facts and habits: I write code. She writes code.~With he, she, it the verb takes s: writes.~The negative uses do not or does not: I do not write.\tShe ___ code. Choose the right word.\twrites\twrite\twriting\t0\tshe takes the verb with s.\tHe __";
    r = r + "_ not like bugs.\tdoes\tdo\tis\t0\the uses does not.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Language: English\tThe past simple\tThe past simple tells what is finished: I wrote a program yesterday.~Regular verbs take ed: played. Irregular verbs change: write -> wrote, run -> ran.~The negative uses did not + the base verb.\tWhat is the past of write?\twrote\twrited\twritten\t0\twrite -> wrote.\tI ___";
    r = r + " not run the test.\tdid\tdo\tdoes\t0\tThe negative past uses did not.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Language: English\tQuestion words\tWhat, Where, When, Why, How start questions.~A question: What does this function do?~An answer: It returns the square of a number.\tWhich word asks for a reason?\tWhy\tWhere\tWhen\t0\tWhy asks for a reason.\tWhere is the file? What does it ask for?\tthe place of the file\tthe";
    r = r + " time\tthe reason\t0\tWhere asks for a place.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Language: English\tProgramming words 1\tvariable, loop, function, array, string.~I declare a variable. The loop repeats ten times.~error, bug, fix: To fix a bug is to correct a mistake in the program.\tWhat does a loop do?\trepeats something\tstores text\tdeletes a file\t0\tA loop repeats.\tTo fix a bug mean";
    r = r + "s...\tto correct a mistake in the program\tto write a new bug\tto delete the file\t0\tfix = correct.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Language: English\tProgramming words 2\tcompile, run, input, output, memory.~The compiler reads the source file. The program prints the output.~CPU, cache.\tWhat is the output of a program?\twhat it prints or gives back\twhat you type\tthe size of memory\t0\tOutput is the result.\tTo compile means...\tto turn";
    r = r + " source code into a program\tto delete code\tto send email\t0\tThe compiler translates.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Language: English\tReading an error message\terror: expected ';' means the compiler wanted a ; at the end of the line.~unknown name 'x' means the compiler does not know the name x.~The line that starts with help: gives advice; always read it.\tWhat does unknown name 'x' mean?\tthe compiler does not know";
    r = r + " the name x\tx is repeated\tx is too big\t0\tunknown = not known.\tWhat does the help: line give?\tadvice on how to fix it\tthe time\tthe name of the author\t0\thelp = advice.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "Language: English\tWriting comments in English\tA comment explains code and starts with //.~Use short sentences: // add two numbers.~Name functions with a verb: count_words, read_file.\tA comment starts with...\t//\t##\tnothing\t0\tJ2K uses // for comments.\tWhich is a good function name?\tread_file\tx1\tthing\t";
    r = r + "0\tA verb tells what it does.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "jcmp: the J2K language\tCompile and run\tjcmp prog.jk -o prog compiles, ./prog runs it.~Without -o the program is named like the file.~jcmp -version tells the version.\tWhich command compiles?\tjcmp prog.jk -o prog\trun prog.jk\t./prog.jk\t0\tjcmp FILE -o NAME.\tHow do you see the version of the compiler?\tjc";
    r = r + "mp -version\tjcmp -show\tjcmp -d\t0\t-version tells it.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "jcmp: the J2K language\tCommon options\t-d checks array indexes while running (slower; use it for tests).~--emit-asm f.jasm writes the assembly that was made, to read.~-novec -nofma -nopeep -noir switch off optimisations to look for a fault.\tWhat does -d do?\tchecks array indexes while running\tmakes it";
    r = r + " faster\tdeletes a file\t0\t-d is the debug build.\tWhich option writes the assembly to read?\t--emit-asm\t-novec\t-d\t0\t--emit-asm writes the text.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "jcmp: the J2K language\tThe types of J2K\tint is always 64 bits; there is no 32-bit int.~double is a 64-bit decimal, float 32 bits, then bool, char, String.~i32 and u32 are for storage in arrays and struct fields.\tHow many bits is an int in J2K?\t64\t32\t16\t0\tint is 64 bits on every target.\tHow is float ";
    r = r + "different from double?\tfloat is 32 bits, double 64 bits\tno difference\tfloat is bigger\t0\tfloat is the smaller one.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "jcmp: the J2K language\tArrays and structs\tint[] a = arr(n) makes a growing array; use a.push(x) and a.len.~struct P { int x; int y; } groups several values.~P p = {1, 2}; then p.x reads a field.\tHow do you add a value at the end of an array?\tpush\tadd_end\tappend_all\t0\ta.push(x).\tWhat is a struct for?";
    r = r + "\tgrouping several values into one\trepeating\tcompiling\t0\tA struct is a set of fields.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "jcmp: the J2K language\tLibraries with #import\t#import <stdlib> then using stdlib; gives jout, str and more.~#import <neuron> is for training models, <simd> for vectors, <time> for time.~Libraries outside the compiler are installed with jc install NAME.\tWhich library gives jout?\tstdlib\tneuron\ttime\t0\t";
    r = r + "#import <stdlib>.\tHow do you install a library that is not part of the compiler?\tjc install NAME\tjcmp -install\tpip\t0\tjc install.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "jcmp: the J2K language\tFunctions and methods\tA plain function: int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }.~A method of a struct uses self: int len2(self) { ... }.~A function marked export can be called from C when you build a .so with -l.\tWhat is export for?\tto be callable from C or Python in a .so\tto ";
    r = r + "make it faster\tto close it\t0\texport marks an entry of the library.\tA method reaches the fields through...\tself\tthis\troot\t0\tJ2K uses self.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "jcmp: the J2K language\tErrors and checks\tAn error looks like file:line:col: error: message.~Use Result and Option so that the program does not fail silently.~-st turns every warning into an error.\tWhat does -st do?\tturns warnings into errors\tmakes it faster\thides messages\t0\tstrict.\tWhat are Option a";
    r = r + "nd Result for?\tto say that a value may be missing or an error may happen, without crashing\tto play games\tto delete files\t0\tThey make failure explicit.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
    r = r + "jcmp: the J2K language\tMaking J2K code fast\tWalk arrays in order, and keep values that do not change in a variable before the loop.~Use float when it is enough, f32x4 for vectors.~Train models with <neuron>, which has the 4x8 kernel; read PERFORMANCE.md.\tWhat helps a loop to be fast?\twalking the arr";
    r = r + "ay in order\tjumping around\tcalling many functions inside\t0\tIn order uses the cache.\tWhat should you use to train a model?\t<neuron>\tjout\t-d\t0\tIt has the fast kernel.\t";
    r = r + "\n";
        return r;
